  "notes": "There are 11 top tricks available (2 spades, the ♥A, the ♦A, and 7 club tricks). A squeeze represents the only hope for trick 12. If West started with 6 or more spades and 4 or more diamonds a simple squeeze against West will function.\n\nSouth should duck the opening lead and, after winning any switch, cash the ♥A and ♦A. If West has the hoped for distribution, he will be squeezed on the run of the clubs.\n\nOn the last club, West must either discard a spade, making the ♠2 a trick, or give up his diamond guard, establishing dummy's ♦4.\n\nOn the given lie of the cards, East also guards the third round of spades. A simple squeeze will no longer work, but a double squeeze will operate if East has both the ♥K and ♥Q.\n\nThe last club winner forces East to pitch a spade. South discards the ♥J and West is squeezed as before.\n\nDouble squeezes generally lead to more complex endings than simple squeezes. Like simple squeezes, double squeezes also mature with 1 loser (making the diamond duck necessary in this problem).\n\nIn every double squeeze both defenders are squeezed in a common suit. That suit must be accompanied by a winner (the ♠A) and have a small card opposite. Each defender also has a suit that he alone can guard (East guards hearts, West guards diamonds). The double squeeze is like two simple squeezes, one against each defender.",
